[0.197, "o", "\u001b[4;1H\u001b[97m\u001b[1m   it packed or obfuscated, and how?\r\n2. Capabilities: what can it actually do? Enumerate its major functional\u001b(B\u001b[m                 \r\n\u001b[97m\u001b[1m   components.\r\n3. Persistence: how does it survive a reboot, if at all?\r\n4. Command and control / networking: protocols, encryption, message formats,\r\n   and any embedded endpoints (domains, IPs, URLs, ports).\r\n5. Cryptography and encoding: algorithms, keys, and what they protect.\r\n   Decode or decrypt embedded strings and configuration where feasible.\r\n6. Anti-analysis: evasion, anti-debug, anti-sandbox, or anti-VM techniques.\r\n7. Injection or privilege-escalation techniques, if any.\r\n8. Indicators of compromise: file paths, registry keys, mutexes, service\r\n    names, domains, IPs, URLs, and any other IOCs recoverable statically.\r\n9. Attribution hints: anything linking it to a known malware family or\r\n   threat actor (shared code, distinctive strings, TTPs). If you beli\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[97m\u001b[1meve you\r\n   know the family, say so and why.\u001b[20;1HBe concrete and exhaustive; prefer decoded facts over speculation, and\r\nclearly mark anything uncertain.\u001b[24;77H\u001b(B\u001b[m           \u001b[C                     \u001b[36m\u001b[1m⠇ \u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[90mThinking…\u001b[26;2H\u001b(B\u001b[m"]
